{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:1dffcd16ebc8c556de465ed889f9c04672bf43e78f9c8af8f7276e742a7b109d","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.95},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.85},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Reference class: 5-yr averages of PPP-weighted OECD+China real growth since 1960, reconstructed with time-varying China PPP weight (China ~33-35% of the combined aggregate today, ~5% in the 1960s, ~12% in the 1990s, ~25-30% in the 2010s). Approximate window averages: 1960s ~5.0%, 1970s ~3.7%, 1980s ~3.6%, 1990s ~3.5%, 2000s ~3.3%, 2010-2019 ~3.5% (0.72*2.0 + 0.28*7.7), 2015-2019 ~3.55%, 2020-2024 ~2.6%, 2022-2024 ~2.7%. Unconditional distribution: mean ~3.4%, sd ~0.8-0.9%, minimum ~2.3-2.6% (2020-24 and 2008-12), maximum ~5.5%. OBSERVED: no 5-year window in 65 years averaged below ~2.3%.\nThis is the one place where the outside view must yield to arithmetic, and I say so explicitly: the reference class is non-stationary in exactly the dimension the locked priors make near-deterministic. Bottom-up from 2A alone — OECD per-capita trend 0.6%/yr plus OECD-38 population ~+0.2-0.3%/yr gives ~0.8-1.1% headline; China at ~2.0-3.0% with a shrinking population and worker/retiree ratio 4.4->2.8. Combined: 0.66*1.0 + 0.34*2.5 = 1.51%. Adding a modest productivity offset of the historically ordinary size (+0.2-0.4pp to OECD) gives 0.66*1.3 + 0.34*2.6 = 1.74%. P50 set at 1.85%, i.e. structurally BELOW the entire historical support of the reference class, because the headcount is not forecastable away.\nDispersion from the reference class rather than from mechanism: the historical sd of realized 5-yr growth averages around a 6-year-ahead central estimate is ~0.8-1.0pp, and ~45% of 5-yr windows contain a G7-synchronized recession costing ~0.5-0.8pp off the average. P10 0.95% (recession window with no offset), P90 2.80% (no recession plus an offset at the upper end of historical technology episodes; note even P90 falls short of the 1960-2019 reference-class MEAN).","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.958Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE Index trailing 24-month average published above 110 at any month-end (after a 2025 close near 64 and a currently-normalized level), sustained for three consecutive month-ends.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ5 conjunct-A marginal of 0.33 was too low against the ~25-30% 10-yr-window base rate; CQ5 moves up from 18 to roughly 30-33. Also pushes CQ4 P50 up ~30-40bp toward the term-premium reference class and shifts ~4-5 points of CQ6 mass from B into C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI annual print at or above 2.0% in two consecutive calendar years, with the household final-consumption share of GDP rising above 42% in the same period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Breaks the Japan-persistence class my 0.72 China-deflation conjunct rests on; CQ2 falls from 36 to roughly 14-18 (0.52 x ~0.30). Raises CQ3 P50 by ~0.15-0.25pp via Chinese nominal demand, and moves ~3 points from CQ6-D toward B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 GDP-weighted core CPI/PCE annual average below 2.0% in three of the four years 2027-2030 — i.e. the early inflationary phase my effort posits simply does not materialize.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my weight-arithmetic claim that shelter + care + services dominate core over the goods-deflation and B2B-disinflation channels; CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.52 to ~0.25 and CQ2 drops from 36 to ~18